# Two MOSFETs, one drive. The same 10 V square, through the same gate resistor, drives a small 2N7000 and a power
# IRFZ44N side by side, each switching the same 100 Ω load from 12 V — the same current, so the only difference on the
# screen is the transistor. What differs is the charge their gates take: the 2N7000's input capacitance is about 20 pF,
# while the IRFZ44N's gate takes 63 nC to reach 10 V, 23 nC of it on the Miller plateau while its drain falls
# (its datasheet's Qg and Qgd). So the 2N7000 switches in nanoseconds and the IRFZ44N in about a microsecond — and with
# a 1 kΩ gate resistor, not within half a period at all — but once on, it drops a millivolt where the 2N7000 drops a
# tenth of a volt.
circuit:two-mosfets("Two MOSFETs, one gate drive") {
    meta {
        summary: "A 2N7000 and an IRFZ44N driven by the same square through the same gate resistor: different thresholds, gate charges and switching times side by side."
        description: """
            Idea: a MOSFET's gate is a capacitor, and the gate resistor fills it. A small transistor has a small gate —
            the 2N7000's input capacitance is about 20 pF — and switches in nanoseconds. A power transistor has a large
            one: the IRFZ44N's datasheet gives 63 nC to bring its gate to 10 V, 23 nC of it on the Miller plateau, where
            the drain is falling and the drive's current goes into the gate–drain capacitance instead of raising the
            gate. That capacitance grows as the drain falls towards the gate, which is why the plateau is long.

            Try: at 100 Ω the IRFZ44N turns on a tenth of a microsecond late and off a whole microsecond late — it must
            give its charge back first. Raise the gate resistor to 1 kΩ: 63 nC through 1 kΩ takes about 12 µs, longer
            than half a period at 50 kHz, and the IRFZ44N never switches fully. Lower the frequency and it recovers.

            Watch: both gates (CH1, CH3) and both drains (CH2, CH4) on one screen. Both loads are 100 Ω, so both
            branches carry the same 120 mA and glow alike on the sheet: what differs is only the transistor. The flat
            step in the IRFZ44N's gate is the plateau; its drain falls during it. Off, the order reverses: the gate
            falls back to the plateau first, sits there while the drain rises, and only then drops away.

            Ask: the drive gives at most (10 V − 5 V plateau) / 100 Ω = 50 mA into the gate. How long does 23 nC take
            at that current? Why does the larger transistor still win once it is fully on?
            """
        category: transistors-as-switches
        tags: transistors, example, tr-04, mosfet, comparison
        authors: "jMouse"
        license: "CERN-OHL-W-2.0"
    }
    parameter:R_gate(100 Ω, in: 10 Ω .. 4.7 kΩ, step: 10 Ω, "Gate resistor, the same for both")
    parameter:f(50 kHz, in: 1 kHz .. 200 kHz, step: 1 kHz, "Drive frequency")
    part:V1(voltage-source, [positive: vdd, negative: 0], U_output: 12 V)
    part:G1(square-generator, [positive: drive, negative: 0], high: 10 V, frequency: f, duty: 0.5)
    part:RG1(resistor, [drive, gate_small], resistance: R_gate)
    part:RG2(resistor, [drive, gate_large], resistance: R_gate)
    part:RL1(resistor, [vdd, drain_small], resistance: 100 Ω, power_rating: 2 W)
    part:RL2(resistor, [vdd, drain_large], resistance: 100 Ω, power_rating: 2 W)
    part:Q1(2n7000, [gate: gate_small, drain: drain_small, source: 0])
    part:Q2(irfz44n, [gate: gate_large, drain: drain_large, source: 0])
    place:V1(at: 240 600, rotate: 90)
    place:G1(at: 480 760, rotate: 90)
    place:RG1(at: 720 600)
    place:RG2(at: 1200 600)
    place:Q1(at: 960 600)
    place:Q2(at: 1440 600)
    place:RL1(at: 1000 320, rotate: 90)
    place:RL2(at: 1480 320, rotate: 90)
    mark:Jd(junction, at: 560 600)
    mark:Jv1(junction, at: 1000 160)
    mark:Jg1(junction, at: 240 920)
    mark:Jg2(junction, at: 480 920)
    mark:Jg3(junction, at: 1000 920)
    mark:Jg4(junction, at: 1480 920)
    mark:GND1(ground, at: 480 1000)
    wire:W1(V1.positive, to: Jv1.j, via: 240 160)
    wire:W2(Jv1.j, to: RL1.positive)
    wire:W3(Jv1.j, to: RL2.positive, via: 1480 160)
    wire:W4(RL1.negative, to: Q1.drain)
    wire:W5(RL2.negative, to: Q2.drain)
    wire:W6(G1.positive, to: Jd.j, via: 480 600)
    wire:W7(Jd.j, to: RG1.positive)
    wire:W8(RG1.negative, to: Q1.gate)
    wire:W9(Jd.j, to: RG2.positive, via: [560 840, 1140 840])
    wire:W10(RG2.negative, to: Q2.gate)
    wire:W11(V1.negative, to: Jg1.j)
    wire:W12(Jg1.j, to: Jg2.j)
    wire:W13(G1.negative, to: Jg2.j)
    wire:W14(Jg2.j, to: GND1.ground)
    wire:W15(Jg2.j, to: Jg3.j)
    wire:W16(Q1.source, to: Jg3.j)
    wire:W17(Jg3.j, to: Jg4.j)
    wire:W18(Q2.source, to: Jg4.j)
    probes {
        probe:CH1(gate_small, 5 V/div)
        probe:CH2(drain_small, 5 V/div)
        probe:CH3(gate_large, 5 V/div)
        probe:CH4(drain_large, 5 V/div)
        time(1 µs/div)
    }
    expect("the 2N7000 follows the drive: its drain is low for half the period", from: "Ciss about 20 pF: through 100 Ω its gate settles in nanoseconds") {
        measure(duty, drain_small, below: 6 V, after: 10 us)
        near(0.5, +- 2%)
    }
    expect("the IRFZ44N lets go a microsecond late: its drain is low for 55 % of the period, not half", from: "Qg 63 nC, Qgd 23 nC: through 100 Ω its gate takes about a microsecond to give back the charge above the plateau and on it") {
        measure(duty, drain_large, below: 6 V, after: 10 us)
        within(0.53 .. 0.57)
    }
    expect("once on, the 2N7000 drops about 0.14 V", from: "RDS(on) 1.2 Ω at VGS 10 V; I = 12 V / 101.2 Ω") {
        measure(minimum, drain_small, after: 10 us)
        within(0.1 V .. 0.2 V)
    }
    expect("once on, the IRFZ44N drops only a couple of millivolts at the same current", from: "RDS(on) at most 17.5 mΩ at VGS 10 V; I = 12 V / 100 Ω") {
        measure(minimum, drain_large, after: 10 us)
        within(1 mV .. 3 mV)
    }
    analysis(transient, for: 60 us)
    view:transient_response(primary)
}
